The fingerprint sensor: loading a trusted app from mainline (QSEOS_APP_START_COMMAND)

WHY
---
The fingerprint sensor is only reachable through its trusted app. Mainline knows
how to FIND a loaded app and SEND it commands:

    qcom_scm_qseecom_app_get_id(name, &id)
    qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(id, ...)

but it does not know how to LOAD one, so it only reaches those already started by
the boot chain -- on this phone, only qcom.tz.uefisecapp, which is where the 29
EFI variables come from. Measured: goodixfp, fpctzapp and keymaster are not
loaded.

This adds the loading, which is what the downstream driver calls
QSEOS_APP_START_COMMAND. The three arguments and their meaning come from
drivers/misc/qseecom.c in Xiaomi's kernel (msm-4.14):

    owner = 50 (QSEE_OS)   svc = 1 (APP_MGR)   cmd = 1 (APP_START)
    args[0] = mdt_len   only the .mdt
    args[1] = img_len   .mdt + all the .bNN
    args[2] = PHYSICAL address of the image
    success: result == 0, and data IS the app_id

THE IMAGE FORMAT, WHICH IS NOT THE ONE IT LOOKS LIKE
----------------------------------------------------
QSEECom wants the files GLUED TOGETHER: first the whole .mdt and behind it each
.bNN, with no padding and WITHOUT honouring the p_offset values of the segment
table. It is literally

    cat app.mdt app.b00 app.b01 ... > image

It is NOT an ELF rebuilt by offsets -- that format is the one the other route,
IAppLoader, wants. Using the wrong one fails without saying why. Both scripts are
in huella/: concatenar-trustlet and fusionar-trustlet.

DECISIONS WORTH KNOWING ABOUT
-----------------------------
- The SCM mempool is NOT used: it is limited to 256 KiB and a trustlet is
  megabytes. Coherent memory is requested on the fly, which is also contiguous,
  which is what passing a physical address demands.
- The app's NAME is not sent to the TrustZone. The downstream driver carries it
  in its structure but never puts it in the call: TZ takes it from the image.
- INCOMPLETE is treated as -ENOTSUPP on purpose. It means the app asked for a
  listener while starting and mainline does not implement them; the app is left
  half started and there is nothing useful to return. Better to say so than to
  fake success.
- The APP_MGR command enum goes separately: APP_START is also 1, just like
  APP_SEND, but in ANOTHER service. Putting them together would be stating
  something false.

TALKING TO IT
-------------
With the app_id the load returns you can send it commands, which is what mainline
ALREADY knew how to do (qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send). The format comes from
disassembling fpc_tac_transfer in the vendor HAL:

    64-byte request, with the command ID in the first word
    64-byte response, right behind it (request + 0x40)

    echo "3 1" > /sys/kernel/debug/probe_ta/hablar

The response is dumped whole to the log because the TA's structures are not known
yet: seeing it raw is precisely the point.

MIND THE BUFFERS: qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send() translates addresses with
qcom_tzmem_to_phys(), which is ONLY valid for TrustZone pool memory. A kmalloc
buffer gives a physical address that is not the right one, and the call fails with
-EINVAL and no further explanation. That is why the wrapper lives in qcom_scm.c,
which is where that pool is.

CUSTOM REQUESTS
---------------
The TA rejects any command from 0 to 24 with the same code (0xe8500029), which
means the request is missing structure, not that the command is unknown. Finding
out what it expects means trying formats, and recompiling a kernel per attempt is
no way to explore. That is why there is a raw mode that sends the bytes as they
are:

    echo "3 0c00000000000000 04000000 01000000" > /sys/kernel/debug/probe_ta/crudo

A hint comes from the HAL: its calls reserve data+12 and write a 12-byte header
-- {0x0000000c, 0x00000000, length} -- before the data. The first field is the
size of the header itself.

HOW IT IS TRIGGERED
-------------------
    cp fpctzapp.img /lib/firmware/
    echo "fpctzapp.img 7260" > /sys/kernel/debug/probe_ta/load
    dmesg | tail

RISK
----
The call may return an error, and that is information. If the TA starts and asks
for a listener, it is left half-done: it is best to reboot before drawing
conclusions from a second test.

--- a/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c
@@ -116,6 +116,15 @@
 	QSEECOM_TZ_CMD_APP_LOOKUP		= 3,
 };
 
+/*
+ * Commands of the app manager service (QSEECOM_TZ_SVC_APP_MGR). Note that
+ * APP_START is also 1: same number, different service, so it cannot live in
+ * the enum above without meaning something else.
+ */
+enum qcom_scm_qseecom_tz_cmd_app_mgr {
+	QSEECOM_TZ_CMD_APP_START		= 1,
+};
+
 enum qcom_scm_qseecom_tz_cmd_info {
 	QSEECOM_TZ_CMD_INFO_VERSION		= 3,
 };
@@ -2282,6 +2291,533 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send);
 
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_qseecom_app_load() - Load a QSEE application into the TrustZone.
+ * @img:     Image to load: the .mdt file followed by every .bNN, concatenated.
+ * @mdt_len: Size of the .mdt part alone.
+ * @img_len: Size of the whole concatenation.
+ * @app_id:  Output, the id the TrustZone assigns to the loaded app.
+ *
+ * Mainline can look an app up and send it commands, but until now could not
+ * load one, so only apps the boot chain had already started were reachable.
+ * This adds the load, which is what the downstream driver calls
+ * QSEOS_APP_START_COMMAND.
+ *
+ * The image is passed by physical address and TrustZone reads it directly, so
+ * the buffer must be physically contiguous. The name is deliberately not sent:
+ * TrustZone takes it from the image itself.
+ *
+ * Return: Zero on success, negative errno on failure.
+ */
+int qcom_scm_qseecom_app_load(const void *img, size_t mdt_len, size_t img_len,
+			      u32 *app_id)
+{
+	struct qcom_scm_qseecom_resp res = {};
+	struct qcom_scm_desc desc = {};
+	dma_addr_t img_phys;
+	void *img_buf;
+	int status;
+
+	if (!img || !img_len || mdt_len > img_len)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/*
+	 * Not the SCM mempool: that one is capped at 256K and a trusted app is
+	 * several megabytes.
+	 */
+	img_buf = dma_alloc_coherent(__scm->dev, img_len, &img_phys, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!img_buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	memcpy(img_buf, img, img_len);
+
+	desc.owner = QSEECOM_TZ_OWNER_QSEE_OS;
+	desc.svc = QSEECOM_TZ_SVC_APP_MGR;
+	desc.cmd = QSEECOM_TZ_CMD_APP_START;
+	desc.arginfo = QCOM_SCM_ARGS(3);
+	desc.args[0] = mdt_len;
+	desc.args[1] = img_len;
+	desc.args[2] = img_phys;
+
+	status = qcom_scm_qseecom_call(&desc, &res);
+
+	dma_free_coherent(__scm->dev, img_len, img_buf, img_phys);
+
+	if (status)
+		return status;
+
+	/*
+	 * INCOMPLETE means the app asked for a listener while starting, and
+	 * mainline has no listener support: the app is half-started and there
+	 * is nothing useful to return. Say so rather than pretend.
+	 */
+	if (res.result == QSEECOM_RESULT_INCOMPLETE)
+		return -ENOTSUPP;
+
+	if (res.result != QSEECOM_RESULT_SUCCESS)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	*app_id = res.data;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_qseecom_app_load);
+
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_qseecom_app_talk() - Send one command to a loaded QSEE app.
+ * @app_id:   Id returned when the app was loaded.
+ * @cmd:      Command id, placed in the first word of the request.
+ * @rsp:      Buffer for the reply, copied out on success.
+ * @rsp_size: Size of @rsp.
+ *
+ * qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send() resolves its buffers with qcom_tzmem_to_phys(),
+ * which only works for memory from the TrustZone pool -- a kmalloc'd buffer
+ * yields a bogus physical address and the call fails with -EINVAL. That pool
+ * is private to this file, hence this wrapper.
+ *
+ * The 64/64 layout comes from the vendor HAL's fpc_tac_transfer: a 64-byte
+ * request whose first word is the command, and a 64-byte response after it.
+ *
+ * Return: Zero on success, negative errno on failure.
+ */
+int qcom_scm_qseecom_app_talk(u32 app_id, u32 cmd, void *rsp, size_t rsp_size)
+{
+	const size_t req_len = 64;
+	const size_t rsp_len = 64;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!rsp || rsp_size < rsp_len)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	void *buf __free(qcom_tzmem) = qcom_tzmem_alloc(__scm->mempool,
+							req_len + rsp_len,
+							GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	memset(buf, 0, req_len + rsp_len);
+	*(u32 *)buf = cmd;
+
+	ret = qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(app_id, buf, req_len,
+					buf + req_len, rsp_len);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	memcpy(rsp, buf + req_len, rsp_len);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_qseecom_app_talk);
+
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_qseecom_app_raw() - Send an arbitrary request to a loaded QSEE app.
+ * @app_id:   Id returned when the app was loaded.
+ * @req:      Request bytes, copied into TrustZone memory.
+ * @req_size: Size of @req.
+ * @rsp:      Buffer for the reply.
+ * @rsp_size: Size of @rsp.
+ *
+ * app_talk() sends a fixed 64/64 layout, which is what the vendor HAL uses for
+ * its simplest calls. Working out what else a trusted app expects means trying
+ * different request shapes, and recompiling a kernel per attempt is no way to
+ * explore. This takes the bytes as given.
+ *
+ * Return: Zero on success, negative errno on failure.
+ */
+int qcom_scm_qseecom_app_raw(u32 app_id, const void *req, size_t req_size,
+			     void *rsp, size_t rsp_size)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!req || !req_size || !rsp || !rsp_size)
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if (req_size + rsp_size > SZ_16K)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	void *buf __free(qcom_tzmem) = qcom_tzmem_alloc(__scm->mempool,
+							req_size + rsp_size,
+							GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	memcpy(buf, req, req_size);
+	memset(buf + req_size, 0, rsp_size);
+
+	ret = qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(app_id, buf, req_size,
+					buf + req_size, rsp_size);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	memcpy(rsp, buf + req_size, rsp_size);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_qseecom_app_raw);
+
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_qseecom_app_fpc() - Send an FPC-style two-buffer command.
+ * @app_id:  Id of the loaded app.
+ * @group:   FPC command group.
+ * @cmd:     Command within the group.
+ * @extra:   Extra words appended after the header, may be NULL.
+ * @n_extra: How many extra words.
+ * @out:     Receives the command buffer after the call.
+ * @out_len: Size of @out in bytes.
+ *
+ * FPC's trusted app does not read the command from the request. The 64-byte
+ * request is a pointer wrapper:
+ *
+ *     struct { u32 length; u32 v_addr; }
+ *
+ * and the command itself lives in a second buffer:
+ *
+ *     struct { u32 group_id; u32 cmd_id; u32 ret_val; }
+ *
+ * On Android, send_modified_cmd hands the second buffer to the TrustZone as an
+ * ION fd and the driver overwrites the word at offset 4 with its physical
+ * address. Mainline has no such variant -- but the TrustZone memory pool is
+ * already shared with the secure world, so a buffer taken from it has a
+ * physical address the TrustZone can follow, and we can write it there
+ * ourselves.
+ *
+ * Return: Zero on success, negative errno on failure.
+ */
+int qcom_scm_qseecom_app_fpc(u32 app_id, u32 group, u32 cmd,
+			     const u32 *extra, size_t n_extra,
+			     void *out, size_t out_len)
+{
+	const size_t req_len = 64;
+	const size_t rsp_len = 64;
+	const size_t cmd_len = PAGE_SIZE;
+	unsigned long cmdpage;
+	phys_addr_t cmd_phys;
+	u32 *req, *cmdbuf;
+	size_t i;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (out_len > cmd_len)
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if (n_extra > 8)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	void *buf __free(qcom_tzmem) = qcom_tzmem_alloc(__scm->mempool,
+							req_len + rsp_len,
+							GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	/*
+	 * The command buffer is deliberately NOT taken from the TrustZone
+	 * pool: v_addr is 32 bits wide, and the pool can sit above 4G, in
+	 * which case the address handed over is a truncated one pointing
+	 * somewhere else entirely. GFP_DMA32 keeps the page addressable.
+	 *
+	 * Getting this wrong is expensive rather than merely wrong. A trustlet
+	 * that faults on a bad address is killed by QSEE, and from then on it
+	 * cannot even be loaded again -- every call returns -EINVAL until the
+	 * phone reboots. Measured, not feared.
+	 */
+	cmdpage = __get_free_pages(GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_ZERO | GFP_DMA32, 0);
+	if (!cmdpage)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	cmdbuf = (u32 *)cmdpage;
+	cmd_phys = virt_to_phys(cmdbuf);
+
+	memset(buf, 0, req_len + rsp_len);
+	req = buf;
+
+	cmdbuf[0] = group;
+	cmdbuf[1] = cmd;
+	for (i = 0; i < n_extra; i++)
+		cmdbuf[3 + i] = extra ? extra[i] : 0;
+
+	/* The wrapper: size of the command buffer, then where it lives. */
+	req[0] = cmd_len;
+	req[1] = lower_32_bits(cmd_phys);
+
+	dev_info(__scm->dev, "fpc: command buffer at %pa (v_addr %#x)\n",
+		 &cmd_phys, req[1]);
+
+	ret = qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(app_id, req, req_len,
+					buf + req_len, rsp_len);
+
+	/* The TA writes its answer back into the command buffer. */
+	if (!ret) {
+		if (out && out_len)
+			memcpy(out, cmdbuf, out_len);
+		ret = *(s32 *)(buf + req_len);
+	}
+
+	free_pages(cmdpage, 0);
+	return ret;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_qseecom_app_fpc);
+
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_qseecom_app_fpc_buf() - Send an FPC command carrying a payload.
+ * @app_id:  Id of the loaded app.
+ * @group:   FPC command group.
+ * @cmd:     Command within the group.
+ * @data:    Payload placed after the header.
+ * @len:     Payload size in bytes.
+ * @out:     Receives the command buffer after the call, may be NULL.
+ * @out_len: Size of @out in bytes.
+ *
+ * Same two-buffer protocol as qcom_scm_qseecom_app_fpc(), but the command
+ * buffer is FPC's longer form:
+ *
+ *     struct { u32 group_id; u32 cmd_id; s32 status; u32 length; u8 data[]; }
+ *
+ * which is what the trusted app expects whenever a command carries more than
+ * its own id -- setting the key, loading a database, reading a template out.
+ *
+ * Return: The app's status word, or negative errno on failure.
+ */
+int qcom_scm_qseecom_app_fpc_buf(u32 app_id, u32 group, u32 cmd,
+				 const void *data, size_t len,
+				 void *out, size_t out_len)
+{
+	const size_t req_len = 64;
+	const size_t rsp_len = 64;
+	const size_t cmd_len = PAGE_SIZE;
+	const size_t header = 16;
+	unsigned long cmdpage;
+	phys_addr_t cmd_phys;
+	u32 *req, *cmdbuf;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (len > cmd_len - header || out_len > cmd_len)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	void *buf __free(qcom_tzmem) = qcom_tzmem_alloc(__scm->mempool,
+							req_len + rsp_len,
+							GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	cmdpage = __get_free_pages(GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_ZERO | GFP_DMA32, 0);
+	if (!cmdpage)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	cmdbuf = (u32 *)cmdpage;
+	cmd_phys = virt_to_phys(cmdbuf);
+
+	memset(buf, 0, req_len + rsp_len);
+	req = buf;
+
+	cmdbuf[0] = group;
+	cmdbuf[1] = cmd;
+	cmdbuf[2] = 0;
+	cmdbuf[3] = len;
+	if (data && len)
+		memcpy((u8 *)cmdbuf + header, data, len);
+
+	req[0] = cmd_len;
+	req[1] = lower_32_bits(cmd_phys);
+
+	ret = qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(app_id, req, req_len,
+					buf + req_len, rsp_len);
+	if (!ret) {
+		if (out && out_len)
+			memcpy(out, cmdbuf, out_len);
+		ret = *(s32 *)(buf + req_len);
+	}
+
+	free_pages(cmdpage, 0);
+	return ret;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_qseecom_app_fpc_buf);
+
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix() - Send a command to Goodix's trusted app.
+ * @app_id:  Id of the loaded app.
+ * @arg0:    First field of its command struct.
+ * @cmd:     Command id.
+ * @data:    Payload, may be NULL.
+ * @len:     Payload size.
+ * @out:     Receives the reply, may be NULL.
+ * @out_len: Size of @out, at most 64.
+ *
+ * Goodix's trusted app speaks a different dialect from FPC's, and the layout
+ * is not a guess: it comes straight from the arguments libgf_ca.so passes to
+ * QSEECom_send_modified_cmd, plus the ION descriptor it hands over with it.
+ *
+ *     send_buf = base,        length 0x80
+ *     resp_buf = base + 0x80, length 0x40
+ *     ifd_data.data[0].cmd_buf_offset = 0
+ *
+ * That last line is the whole point. A cmd_buf_offset of zero tells Android's
+ * qseecom driver to overwrite the FIRST word of the request with the physical
+ * address of the shared buffer -- so word 0 is a pointer the app follows, and
+ * whatever userspace wrote there is a placeholder. It is the same trick FPC's
+ * app uses at offset 4, which is why a request built by hand from userspace
+ * can never work for either of them: only the kernel knows the address.
+ *
+ * The command struct then sits at base + 8, with arg0 at +4 and the command id
+ * at +0x18.
+ *
+ * Return: The app's reply status, or negative errno on failure.
+ */
+int qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix(u32 app_id, u32 arg0, u32 cmd,
+				const void *data, size_t len,
+				void *out, size_t out_len)
+{
+	const size_t req_len = 0x80;
+	const size_t rsp_len = 0x40;
+	const size_t data_off = 0x40;
+	u32 *req, *command;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (out_len > req_len || len > req_len - data_off)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/*
+	 * Request and reply share one allocation, contiguous, because the app
+	 * is handed a single buffer and told the reply lives 0x80 bytes into
+	 * it. Splitting them would put the reply somewhere it will not look.
+	 */
+	void *buf __free(qcom_tzmem) = qcom_tzmem_alloc(__scm->mempool,
+							req_len + rsp_len,
+							GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	memset(buf, 0, req_len + rsp_len);
+
+	req = buf;
+	command = buf + 8;
+
+	/* Word 0: the address the app dereferences. */
+	req[0] = lower_32_bits(qcom_tzmem_to_phys(buf));
+
+	command[1] = arg0;		/* base + 0x0c */
+	command[6] = cmd;			/* base + 0x20 */
+	command[7] = len;			/* base + 0x24 */
+	if (data && len)
+		memcpy((u8 *)buf + data_off, data, len);
+
+	ret = qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(app_id, req, req_len,
+					buf + req_len, rsp_len);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * Two different things live in two different places, and conflating
+	 * them is what produced a uniform "reply" before. The command's status
+	 * word is at resp_buf + 4 -- that is where libgf_ca.so reads its return
+	 * value (ldr w, [resp_buf, #4]). Any DATA the app writes back lands in
+	 * the shared command buffer instead. So: return the status from
+	 * resp_buf+4, and hand the caller the command buffer to inspect.
+	 */
+	if (out && out_len)
+		memcpy(out, buf, out_len);
+
+	return *(s32 *)(buf + req_len + 4);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix);
+
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix_raw() - Send a caller-built Goodix request.
+ * @app_id:  Id of the loaded app.
+ * @in:      The 0x80-byte request, built by the caller.
+ * @out:     Receives the command buffer after the call (0x80 bytes).
+ * @status:  Receives the status word from resp_buf+4.
+ *
+ * Same transport as qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix(), but every field of the
+ * request comes from the caller -- token, sequence, timestamps, the lot. The
+ * kernel only stamps word zero with the buffer's physical address, which is
+ * the one field userspace cannot know. This exists because Goodix's session is
+ * stateful with many fields, and probing them one recompile at a time is no way
+ * to find them; here the whole struct is under userspace control at once.
+ *
+ * Return: Zero on success, negative errno on failure.
+ */
+int qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix_raw(u32 app_id, const void *in,
+				    void *out, s32 *status)
+{
+	const size_t req_len = 0x80;
+	const size_t rsp_len = 0x40;
+	u32 *req;
+	int ret;
+
+	void *buf __free(qcom_tzmem) = qcom_tzmem_alloc(__scm->mempool,
+							req_len + rsp_len,
+							GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	memset(buf, 0, req_len + rsp_len);
+	memcpy(buf, in, req_len);
+
+	req = buf;
+	/* NO word0 stamp: command is self-contained (verified in emulator); */
+	/* stamping makes the TA 2nd MAC over word0..3 fail and return -1.   */
+	ret = qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(app_id, req, req_len,
+					buf + req_len, rsp_len);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	if (out)
+		memcpy(out, buf, rsp_len); /* TA writes its response IN-PLACE over the request (buf+0) */
+	if (status)
+		*status = *(s32 *)(buf + 4);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix_raw);
+
+/*
+ * qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix_mod() - Modified command with a data buffer.
+ * Lays out tzmem as [cmd 0x80][rsp 0x40][data], stamps the command at
+ * @cmd_buf_offset with the physical address of the data region -- this is what
+ * QSEECom's ifd/cmd_buf_offset does for commands that reference a buffer (e.g.
+ * DETECT_SENSOR). Session commands carry no data buffer and must NOT stamp.
+ * Hands back the in-place command response and the data region the TA wrote.
+ */
+int qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix_mod(u32 app_id, const void *cmd, void *cmd_out,
+				    void *data, size_t datalen,
+				    u32 cmd_buf_offset, s32 *status)
+{
+	const size_t req_len = 0x80;
+	const size_t rsp_len = 0x40;
+	size_t total = req_len + rsp_len + datalen;
+	phys_addr_t dphys;
+	u32 *req;
+	int ret;
+
+	void *buf __free(qcom_tzmem) = qcom_tzmem_alloc(__scm->mempool,
+							total, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	memset(buf, 0, total);
+	memcpy(buf, cmd, req_len);
+	if (datalen)
+		memcpy(buf + req_len + rsp_len, data, datalen);
+
+	req = buf;
+	dphys = qcom_tzmem_to_phys(buf + req_len + rsp_len);
+	*(u32 *)((u8 *)buf + cmd_buf_offset) = lower_32_bits(dphys);
+	pr_info("probe_ta: goodixmod word0[%u]=%#x data=%zu bytes\n",
+		cmd_buf_offset, *(u32 *)((u8 *)buf + cmd_buf_offset), datalen);
+
+	ret = qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(app_id, req, req_len,
+					buf + req_len, rsp_len);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	if (cmd_out)
+		memcpy(cmd_out, buf, req_len);
+	if (data && datalen)
+		memcpy(data, buf + req_len + rsp_len, datalen);
+	if (status)
+		*status = *(s32 *)((u8 *)buf + 4);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_qseecom_app_goodix_mod);
